 "news": {
  "date": "2026-10-20",
  "asof": "2026-10-21 09:49 UTC",
  "headline": "France got worse and American yields fell \u2014 because the number that moved them was housing",
  "standfirst": "The money went where the AI money is not: the Dow rose 1.02%, 529.85 points, and the Russell 2000 1.65%, while the Nasdaq fell 0.02% and the Philadelphia semiconductor index 0.29% with 19 of its 30 members lower. Meta fell 1.63%, a fifth straight losing session, and reports after Wednesday's close. The S&P managed +0.28% on earnings from Equifax and Hasbro and a rally in 3M; GM fell 7%. That is the second time in four sessions this pair has led. The bond market broke Monday's story: France deteriorated \u2014 the ten-year OAT at 4.93%, the spread over Germany 8.5bp wider at 150.8, 2,400-plus arrests, more curfews, a Moody's review on Friday \u2014 and US Treasuries rallied: thirty-year \u22126bp to 5.42%, ten-year \u22123bp to 5.12%, two-year +2bp to 4.79%. What moved them was American: September housing starts \u22125.2% to a 1.221m pace against 1.305m expected, with single-family permits at a three-and-a-half-year low and the thirty-year mortgage at 7.40%. Crude reversed off a five-month high \u2014 Brent $108.79, WTI's expiring November contract $101.79 \u2014 after Iran's president met Pakistan's army chief, while Hormuz crude flows recovered to about 10.3m barrels a day, 76% of pre-war. In the book OKTA rose 2.15%, closing 5.7% above its low, while GOOG fell 1.11% to its lowest close since 1 October and AAPL 1.00% on a Loop Capital downgrade. A net of -3.11. Heat 3.05R against 3.0R, unchanged for a twenty-ninth session. Apple has dropped back below +1R.",
  "themes": [
   {
    "kicker": "What's next",
    "right": "Meta tonight \u00b7 the foldable Friday \u00b7 then the Fed",
    "title": "Meta tests the AI question before anything in this book does",
    "items": [
     {
      "tone": "c-er",
      "tag": "META",
      "html": "<b>Meta Q3 after the close</b> \u2014 a fifth straight losing session into it; the question is whether full-year capex guidance holds, consensus EPS $5.94",
      "when": "Wed \u00b7 2026-10-21",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-er",
      "tag": "AAPL",
      "html": "<b>iPhone Duo first shipments</b> and retail availability in the US and 70-plus countries, 28 more on the 30th \u2014 the test is whether store stock lasts the morning",
      "when": "Fri \u00b7 2026-10-23",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-fomc",
      "tag": "FRA",
      "html": "<b>Moody's reviews France</b> at Aa3 negative \u2014 a one-notch cut takes it to A1, with the OAT spread already 150.8bp",
      "when": "Fri \u00b7 2026-10-23",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-fomc",
      "tag": "FOMC \u00b7 GOOG",
      "html": "<b>Fed decision and Alphabet Q3 on the same afternoon</b> \u2014 a hold is expected, December is 94% priced for a hike, and Alphabet carries $195-205bn of capex into it",
      "when": "Wed \u00b7 2026-10-28",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-cb",
      "tag": "AAPL",
      "html": "<b>Apple Q4</b> \u2014 a question about mix and margin after the Loop downgrade, with the foldable selling and the Pro's lead times at zero in China",
      "when": "Mon \u00b7 2026-11-02",
      "kind": "calendar"
     }
    ],
    "note": "<b>Tonight is Meta's, and it is the cleanest test of the AI question this week.</b> It reports after Wednesday's close having fallen five sessions in a row, and what is being asked is not whether revenue grew but whether <b>full-year capital-expenditure guidance holds</b>. That is the same question the Nasdaq has been answering and un-answering for four sessions, and it is the question Alphabet faces on the 28th with <b>$195-205bn</b> of its own. Nothing in this book reports tonight, which makes it a free read: a market that punishes Meta for spending will punish Alphabet for spending."
   }
